  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei died on July 2, 2026; Iranian authorities announced a state funeral from July 3-8 and declared an official mourning period across all government institutions. All formally scheduled diplomatic sessions under the Islamabad MOU entered a declared interregnum pause, with Iranian and US officials confirming no working-group meetings would take place before July 9. On July 1, VP Vance had stated in CNN remarks that nuclear-track discussions would convene "soon," but the Nuclear Affairs working group calendar remained unset as of July 4, with Deputy FM Gharibabadi confirming four designated working groups without providing convening dates. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>The July 1-2 Doha session concluded with agreement on a first $6 billion frozen-asset tranche release, confirmed in separate TIER1 statements by both the US and Iranian delegations. The session did not produce language on uranium enrichment levels or HEU stockpile disposition. VP Vance explicitly stated the US "does not recognize any Iranian right to impose transit fees" in a July 1 press appearance, rejecting an Iranian draft fee-schedule proposal for Hormuz passage; the toll dispute remained unresolved at the close of the session. The Nuclear Affairs working group calendar was discussed but not fixed, pending the funeral-week pause. → supports <span class="cite">F12</span> without move</p>

      <p>AIS tracker straits.live logged 43 commercial transits per day at Hormuz on July 1-2, up from 27 on June 28, following the CENTCOM June 16 announcement of active mine countermeasure operations. France deployed minesweepers to the outer Hormuz approaches on July 4, extending coalition coverage to the eastern strait entrance as part of a 15-nation MCM force. P&I insurance clubs had not issued broad commercial war-risk clearances as of July 4; IRGC Marine Division released a July 2 advisory against unauthorized passage through the strait's northern corridor. These competing vectors — AIS recovery partially offset by insurance and IRGC friction — set F10's initial estimate at 38%. → moved <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>Parliamentary speaker Mohammad Bagher Qalibaf stated on state television on July 1 that IAEA inspectors are "strictly barred from accessing any bombed or damaged sites under any circumstances," limiting permissible access to Bushehr and the Tehran research reactor only. The US government's July 2 statement confirmed the second $6 billion frozen-asset tranche remains frozen pending "expanded IAEA access" — a condition Qalibaf's on-record pronouncement structurally blocks in the near term. The Doha session produced no MFA reversal signal; Khamenei funeral-week pause forecloses any revision before July 9. These compounding barriers set F11's initial estimate at 17%. → moved <span class="cite">F11</span></p>

      <p>Knesset legal adviser Sagit Afik formally recommended dissolution on July 17, per Times of Israel reporting on July 3. The operative election window circulating in coalition deliberations — September 8 through October 20 — contains no dates on or after October 27, satisfying F6's resolution criteria if a third reading passes by July 15. As of July 4, coalition whip Ofir Akunis had not published a plenary calendar for second-reading proceedings; Shas and UTJ conditioned support on a binding election-date agreement not concluded as of July 4. F6 p raised 0.45→0.55; F7 staleness override applied raising 0.35→0.80 (derivation: P(F6 YES)×1.0 + P(F6 NO)×0.55). → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

